%% SECTION Business Environment, Tailoring and Toolbox
@@
Which activity should come first when planning project compliance?
A) Identify the applicable legal, regulatory, contractual and policy requirements with experts
B) Schedule the final compliance audit, so that the team knows the date it is working toward from the start
C) Request exemptions from the regulations that are likely to slow the project down
D) Assign compliance to a junior analyst so that senior staff can focus on delivery
= A
? Compliance planning begins with identifying requirements; then they are classified, planned, assigned, monitored and audited.

@@
An internal audit finds that the project has not been storing personal data according to the company's data protection policy. What should the project manager do?
A) Stop the non-compliant practice, inform the right stakeholders, correct it and prevent recurrence
B) Plan to fix the issue during the final phase, when the team will have more of its capacity available
C) Ask the auditor to treat the finding as an observation rather than as a non-compliance
D) Record the finding in the lessons learned register and continue with the current practice
= A
? Non-compliance must be addressed immediately and transparently, with corrective and preventive actions.

@@
Which change model focuses on the psychological transition people experience: endings, the neutral zone and new beginnings?
A) Bridges' transition model
B) Kotter's 8 steps
C) ADKAR
D) Satir change model
= A
? Bridges distinguishes the external change from the internal transition people go through.

@@
In the Satir change model, what typically happens right after a "foreign element" is introduced?
A) Chaos, with a temporary drop in performance
B) Immediate improvement in team performance
C) A new status quo at a higher level of performance
D) A return to the late status quo
= A
? The foreign element causes chaos; after a transforming idea, practice and integration lead to a new status quo.

@@
A new ERP system will change how 3,000 employees work. Which practice best supports adoption?
A) Identify change champions, provide training and communicate the reasons repeatedly
B) Announce the change in a single email to all staff on the go-live date
C) Switch off the old systems without notice, so that all users have no choice but to adopt the new one
D) Leave adoption activities to the IT department, which owns the new system
= A
? Organisational change succeeds with sponsorship, champions, communication, training and reinforcement.

@@
Which of the following best describes a value delivery office (VDO)?
A) A PMO that coaches teams and supports adaptive ways of working with a focus on value
B) A PMO that concentrates on auditing projects for compliance with standard templates
C) A finance unit that approves project budgets and tracks the value of each investment
D) A procurement unit that negotiates agile contracts with external service providers
= A
? A VDO is a PMO adapted to agile environments, focused on enabling value delivery rather than enforcing process.

@@
Which external analysis technique examines political, economic, social, technological, legal and environmental factors?
A) PESTLE
B) SWOT
C) RACI
D) MoSCoW
= A
? PESTLE is a prompt list for scanning the external environment.

@@
A competitor launches a similar product earlier than expected. What should the project manager do first?
A) Analyse the impact on the business case and strategy with the sponsor and product owner
B) Continue as planned, since the project scope and baselines have already been approved
C) Cancel the project immediately, since the competitor has already captured most of the market
D) Ask the team to work double shifts so the product can be launched as soon as possible
= A
? External changes may affect value; the PM assesses impact and supports decision-makers with options.

@@
Which practice best represents continuous improvement in an adaptive team?
A) Retrospectives each iteration that produce specific, tracked actions
B) An annual audit of the team's process by the quality department
C) A lessons learned workshop at the end of the project with all members
D) A new process tried every week so the team can compare as many as possible
= A
? Frequent, actionable retrospectives embed continuous improvement in the team's rhythm.

@@
Which statement about sustainability in projects is correct?
A) It covers environmental, social and economic considerations across the product life cycle
B) It mainly concerns recycling and reducing paper use in the project's offices
C) It applies to construction and energy projects, but only rarely to software or service projects
D) It is the responsibility of the organisation's marketing and communications team
= A
? Sustainability spans the triple bottom line and all project areas, from sourcing to design to team well-being.

@@
An AI tool used to screen candidates for project roles seems to favour applicants from one university. What should the project manager do?
A) Stop relying on the tool for decisions, report the possible bias and ensure a fair human review
B) Continue using the tool, since it is efficient and its results are completely objective by design
C) Keep using the tool but do not mention the pattern, to avoid embarrassing the vendor
D) Recruit from that university only, since the tool has identified the best candidates
= A
? AI can reproduce bias; responsible use requires human oversight, fairness and transparency.

@@
Which statement about AI-generated status reports is most appropriate?
A) They can save time, but the project manager must verify them before distribution
B) They need no review, since AI produces objective and consistent information
C) They should never be used, since stakeholders expect all reports to be written by people
D) They replace the need for conversations with stakeholders about project status
= A
? AI augments the PM's work; accountability for accuracy and appropriateness stays with people.

@@ | multi
Which two items should an organisation establish when adopting AI tools for project work? (Choose two.)
A) Policies defining approved tools and acceptable data use
B) Training to build AI literacy for project staff
C) A rule that AI outputs never need checking
D) Unrestricted use of confidential data in public tools
E) Elimination of human decision-making
= A, B
? AI governance and training enable responsible, effective use; unchecked outputs and uncontrolled data use create risk.

@@
What is the main purpose of tailoring the project approach?
A) To fit methods, processes and governance to the context so that value is delivered effectively
B) To make every project in the organisation identical so that they are easier to compare
C) To give the project manager freedom from any organisational policies that slow down the project's work
D) To add documentation to every project so that audits can be passed without difficulty
= A
? Tailoring adapts the approach to the context while respecting mandatory requirements.

@@
Which factor would most likely lead a team to tailor toward more frequent, smaller deliveries?
A) Highly uncertain requirements where early user feedback is valuable
B) A building foundation that cannot be modified after it has been poured
C) A regulatory submission that must be complete before it can be reviewed
D) A fully specified purchase of standard items from a catalogue
= A
? Uncertainty and the value of feedback favour incremental, adaptive delivery.

@@
A project team must choose an artifact to show the probability and impact ratings of their top risks visually. Which artifact is most suitable?
A) Heat map / probability and impact matrix
B) WBS dictionary
C) Team charter
D) Milestone list
= A
? A heat map or probability-impact matrix displays risk ratings visually for prioritisation.

@@
Which artifact would best help an executive understand where a product is heading over the next four quarters?
A) Product roadmap
B) Activity list
C) Issue log
D) Sprint burndown
= A
? Roadmaps communicate the high-level direction and planned releases over time.

@@
Which statement about benefit owners is correct?
A) They monitor and realise benefits, often after the project has closed
B) They are always members of the project team who report to the PM
C) They approve each work package before the team starts working on it in the project
D) They are responsible for maintaining the project schedule baseline
= A
? Benefit owners, often in the business, track and realise benefits during and after transition.

@@
Users of a new system have learned how to use it but continue reverting to old habits after a month. According to ADKAR, which element needs attention?
A) Reinforcement
B) Awareness
C) Knowledge
D) Desire
= A
? Reinforcement sustains change through recognition, measurement and corrective actions after implementation.

@@
Which type of PMO directly manages projects and to which project managers report?
A) Directive
B) Supportive
C) Controlling
D) Virtual
= A
? Directive PMOs exercise high control by managing projects directly.

@@
During execution, the team discovers that a key impediment requires approval from another department's director. The team cannot resolve it. What should the project manager do?
A) Engage the director, explain the impact and options, and escalate through governance if needed
B) Tell the team to wait until the director notices the problem and resolves it on their own initiative
C) Bypass the approval step so that the team can keep working while the request is pending
D) Remove the dependent work from scope without consulting the product owner or sponsor
= A
? Servant leaders remove impediments beyond the team's control, using governance channels when necessary.

@@
Which indicator is a leading indicator of the future benefit "increased online sales"?
A) Number of users completing the new checkout in the pilot
B) Total online sales revenue reported for the whole of the next year
C) Final project cost compared with the approved budget
D) Number of contracts closed at the end of the project
= A
? Leading indicators predict future outcomes; pilot adoption suggests future sales, which will be measured later as lagging indicators.

@@
The PMO requires a 30-page status report every week for a small, two-month internal project. What is the most appropriate action?
A) Propose a lighter, tailored reporting format to the PMO that still meets governance needs
B) Stop producing the report without telling the PMO, since the project is small
C) Produce the full report by copying content from previous weeks to save effort
D) Escalate the PMO's requirement to the chief executive as an example of excessive bureaucracy
= A
? Tailoring for the organisation involves agreeing proportionate governance with the PMO.

@@
What is the main benefit of documenting tailoring decisions?
A) Transparency about why the approach was chosen, so it can be reviewed and improved
B) A longer project plan, which is generally viewed favourably by governance bodies
C) Evidence for external auditors only, since internal stakeholders rarely read it
D) Protection against future changes, since documented decisions cannot be revisited by anyone
= A
? Documented tailoring supports alignment, governance and continuous improvement.

@@
Which external change would most directly require the project to revisit its compliance plan?
A) A new safety regulation affecting the product's design
B) A team member's holiday
C) A change in the colour of the company logo on the product's packaging
D) A new meeting room booking system
= A
? Regulatory changes directly affect compliance requirements and must be assessed and incorporated.
